      "description": "Temple of Hera in Olympia: figures from the west pediment. Centaur robbing a woman (J, K). Three parts of this group were found already in March 1877 and represented 25 II. The fourth, covering the breast of the Centaurs and the average return of the wife was the first significant archaeological discovery of the third period on 16 Oct. 1877 lifted. It was 25.80 sec and 6.00 sec from the SW corner, away from other parts of the group c. 50 m. A subsequent Attached Hinterschenkelfragment the centaur pl.xxvi XXVI1-J Also from the pieced portion of the finely pleated sleeves a fragment is preserved. Abgespitzte to the spot on the stern of the Centaurs leaned Lapithenschenkel PI XJVb, see Section III H - height 2 m, width 2,35 m.",
